Architecture: SDD

Purpose:
  Define the internal architecture of the .sdd system itself — which
  components exist inside .sdd/, how they relate, and which relationships
  are allowed. This is NOT the project's technical architecture (stack,
  module isolation, layers) — that lives in project/architecture.sdd
  (see Note below — similar name, different scope). It is also NOT the
  global engineering-architecture rules (modularity, coupling, dependency
  direction, service extraction) the AI applies to any project's actual
  code — those live in the sibling file ./principles.sdd (see Note below,
  STEP 13 entry).

Owns:
  .sdd/

Components:

  PROJECT:
    path: ../PROJECT.sdd
    role: Root entry point / router (see PROJECT.sdd -> Rules R1-R3)

  PROJECT_MODEL:
    path: ../project
    role: Project knowledge model (WHAT/WHY/WHERE)

  ARCHITECTURE:
    path: ../architecture
    role: SDD system architecture definitions (this file, the component
      graph) plus the global engineering-architecture principles engine
      (./principles.sdd — modularity, coupling, dependency direction,
      service extraction; STEP 13)

  CHAINS:
    path: ../chains
    role: Execution chains (chain-type model — feature/bugfix/change, STEP 5)

  SKILLS:
    path: ../skills
    role: Engineering skills (HOW, STEP 6)

  PROMPTS:
    path: ../prompts
    role: Prompt intelligence (inbox, analysis, STEP 7)

  TASKS:
    path: ../tasks/tasks.sdd
    role: Task ENGINE (HOW a task works — lifecycle, creation, decomposition,
      dependency, state, review, failure, recovery, relocation, completion).
      Root-level, because this is .sdd's own system behavior, not project
      knowledge (portability). Concrete task instances ("what does it belong
      to?") live under their own domain (../project/<domain>/tasks/*.sdd),
      not in this component (STEP 8 final, see ../tasks/tasks.sdd -> Note)

  DECISIONS:
    path: ../decisions
    role: Human decision ENGINE (decision lifecycle, Rules [D1]-[D17],
      STEP 9 enrichment). Root-level, same portability reasoning as TASKS above
      — this is .sdd's own decision-management behavior, not project
      knowledge. Concrete decision instances live under their owning
      domain (../project/<domain>/decisions/<state>/*.sdd), not in this
      component (see ../decisions/decisions.sdd -> Note)

  STATE:
    path: ../state
    role: State notation ENGINE (universal state symbols, meanings and
      transition rules — Symbols/ExtendedStates/Meaning/Flows/Rules
      [S1]-[S16], STEP 10). Root-level, same portability reasoning as TASKS
      and DECISIONS above — this defines .sdd's own state vocabulary, not
      project knowledge. Stores no per-object state value: each object
      keeps its own state inline in its own `State:` field (see
      ../state/state.sdd -> Storage)


Relationships:

  PROJECT
    -> PROJECT_MODEL

  PROJECT
    -> ARCHITECTURE

  PROJECT_MODEL
    -> CHAINS

  CHAINS
    -> SKILLS

  PROMPTS
    -> TASKS

  TASKS
    -> STATE

  DECISIONS
    -> ARCHITECTURE
    -> PROJECT_MODEL
    -> CHAINS


Allowed:

  PROJECT:
    may_reference:
      PROJECT_MODEL
      ARCHITECTURE
      CHAINS
      SKILLS
      PROMPTS
      TASKS
      DECISIONS
      STATE

  PROJECT_MODEL:
    may_reference:
      ARCHITECTURE
      CHAINS
      SKILLS
      TASKS
      DECISIONS

  CHAINS:
    may_reference:
      SKILLS
      TASKS
      STATE
      DECISIONS

  TASKS:
    may_reference:
      PROJECT_MODEL
      CHAINS
      SKILLS
      DECISIONS
      STATE

  PROMPTS:
    may_reference:
      PROJECT_MODEL
      ARCHITECTURE
      CHAINS
      SKILLS
      TASKS
      DECISIONS


Forbidden:

  SKILLS:
    MUST NOT redefine PROJECT architecture.

  TASKS:
    MUST NOT redefine global SDD architecture.

  PROMPTS:
    MUST NOT silently modify architecture or global rules.

  STATE:
    MUST NOT define architecture.

  PROJECT_MODEL:
    MUST NOT contain engineering skill instructions.

  ARCHITECTURE:
    MUST NOT contain implementation code.


Principles:

  SeparationOfConcerns:
    Each SDD component MUST own one responsibility.

  SingleSourceOfTruth:
    A fact MUST have one authoritative SDD owner.

  ExplicitReference:
    Cross-component knowledge MUST use references.

  Locality:
    Detailed knowledge MUST live as close as possible to
    the component that owns it.

  NoDuplication:
    The same knowledge MUST NOT be copied across multiple SDD files.

  Traceability:
    Every task MUST be traceable to a project element,
    chain stage or decision.

  HumanControl:
    Global architecture changes require human approval — this is the
    same rule as PROJECT.sdd -> Rules [R9]/[R11], not a separate
    definition; this file does not restate it as new authority, it just
    applies it to the SDD component graph above.

  TokenEconomy:
    AI MUST read the smallest sufficient context for a task, and MUST
    expand a reference (follow it to another SDD file) only when the
    task actually requires that file's detail — this is the same
    Locality/ExplicitReference discipline above, applied to how AI
    consumes the graph at read-time, not a new authority.

  CompactModel:
    SDD content itself MUST stay compact and operational (rules, not
    prose) — the full narrative explanation of a decision belongs to
    human-facing documentation (e.g. README.md's per-STEP log), which
    MAY restate an SDD rule at length for a human reader without that
    restatement becoming a second SSOT for the rule. (e.g. BDD
    scenarios: AI-facing form uses abbreviated `G:`/`W:`/`T:` tags,
    human-facing form uses full `Given`/`When`/`Then` prose — same
    semantics, not two sources of truth; STEP 24.)

  CleanSource:
    Real source directories (once they exist) MUST NOT contain
    co-located SDD or doc metadata files next to code (no
    `repository.sdd`/`repository.md` sitting beside `repository.go`) —
    AI-facing semantic data lives only under the mirrored `.sdd/project`
    tree (bax project/map.sdd -> CodeReference -> Mirror), human-facing
    narrative only under `docs/` (bax project/map.sdd -> Paths). This is
    an application of Locality/SeparationOfConcerns above, not a new
    authority (STEP 24).

  DiagramAsOutput:
    A diagram (Mermaid/PlantUML/Graphviz, etc.) MUST be generated FROM
    the project model — it is a rendering, never a second source of
    truth (bax SingleSourceOfTruth above). AI MUST NOT derive or update
    the project model from a diagram (STEP 24).

State: +
  (STEP 3 completed — see PROJECT.sdd -> Rules [R10]: state must be marked
  when a stage finishes.)

Note:
  (prompt/new/20.md) This file describes how .sdd's OWN components
  (PROJECT, PROJECT_MODEL, ARCHITECTURE, CHAINS, SKILLS, PROMPTS, TASKS,
  DECISIONS, STATE) relate to each other — it operates at the "meta" level.

  Important distinction (the names collide): `project/architecture.sdd` is
  the PROJECT's own technical architecture (stack, ModuleIsolation,
  LayerArchitecture — how FE/BE/DB call each other) and is COMPLETELY
  separate from this file. This file never duplicates that content (see
  NoDuplication above) — it only references PROJECT_MODEL as a component.

  (prompt/new/21.md, STEP 4 — RESOLVED) The stale `protocol/symbols.sdd`
  and `protocol/rules.sdd` references that `project/architecture.sdd` used
  to carry were removed by STEP 4's Full Reset; that file is now
  schema-only and carries no remaining reference to the retired
  `protocol/` tree (see .sdd/project/architecture.sdd -> Note,
  .sdd/PROJECT.sdd -> Note).

